222
00:15:26,320 --> 00:15:28,600
now and where you’ve been..

223
00:15:28,600 --> 00:15:34,560
You’ve all gotten a little taller,, a little stronger,, a little smarter, I hope.”.

224
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:37,400
Here some people in the audience chuckled..

225
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:41,640
“But the best way to measure how you’ve grown isn’t by inches or the number of

226
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:46,480
laps you can now run around the track, or even your grade point average.

227
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:49,760
Those those things are important, to be sure..

228
00:15:49,760 --> 00:15:53,760
It’s what you’ve done with your time,, how you’ve chosen to spend your days,,

229
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:56,200
and whom you have touched this year..

230
00:15:56,200 --> 00:16:00,440
That, to me, is the greatest measure of success..

231
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:02,560
“There’s a wonderful line in a book by J..

232
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:06,080
M.. Barrie—. —and no,, it’s not Peter Pan,

233
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:09,960
and I’m not going to ask you to clap if you believe in fairies..

234
00:16:09,960 --> 00:16:12,560
Here everyone laughed again..

235
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:19,040
“But in another book by J.. M.. Barrie called The Little White Bird, he writes,

236
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:23,120
He started flipping through a small book on the podium until he found the page he

237
00:16:23,120 --> 00:16:27,240
was looking for,. and then he put on his reading glasses..

238
00:16:27,240 --> 00:16:29,880
“‘Shall we make a new rule of life?

239
00:16:29,880 --> 00:16:34,800
always to try to be a little kinder than is necessary?’”.

240
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:38,160
Here Mr.. Tushman looked up at the audience..

241
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:41,760
“Kinder than is necessary,”, he repeated..

242
00:16:41,760 --> 00:16:44,920
“What a marvelous line,, isn’t it??

243
00:16:44,920 --> 00:16:47,960
Kinder than is necessary.

244
00:16:47,960 --> 00:16:50,960
Because it’s not enough to be kind..

245
00:16:50,960 --> 00:16:53,760
One should be kinder than needed..

246
00:16:53,760 --> 00:16:59,160
Why I love that line,, that concept,, is that it reminds me that we carry with

247
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:07,040
us, as human beings, not just the capacity to be kind,, but the very choice of kindness..

248
00:17:07,040 --> 00:17:08,720
And what does that mean??

249
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:10,360
How is that measured??

250
00:17:10,360 --> 00:17:12,560
You can’t use a yardstick..

251
00:17:12,560 --> 00:17:15,199
It’s like I was saying just before:.

252
00:17:15,199 --> 00:17:18,720
it’s not like measuring how much you’ve grown in a year..

253
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:21,640
It’s not exactly quantifiable,, is it??

254
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:27,079
How do we know we’ve been kind?? What is being kind, anyway?”?

255
00:17:27,079 --> 00:17:32,280
He put on his reading glasses again and started flipping through another small book..

256
00:17:32,280 --> 00:17:37,120
“There’s another passage in a different book I’d like to share with you,”, he said.,

257
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:39,640
“If you’ll bear with me until I find it..

258
00:17:39,640 --> 00:17:44,840
Ah,, here we go. And Under the Eye of the Clock by Christopher Nolan,, the main

259
00:17:44,840 --> 00:17:49,680
character is a young man who is facing some extraordinary challenges..

260
00:17:49,680 --> 00:17:52,400
There’s this one part where someone helps him:.

261
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:58,880
a kid in his class.. On the surface,, it’s a small gesture., But to this young man,,

262
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:03,360
whose name is Joseph,, it’s well,, if you’ll permit me.

263
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:06,560
He cleared his throat and read from the book:.

264
00:18:06,560 --> 00:18:10,800
“‘It was at moments such as these that Joseph recognized the face of God in

265
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:15,480
human form.. It glimmered in their kindness to him,. it glowed in their

266
00:18:15,480 --> 00:18:18,640
keenness,. it hinted in their caring,.

267
00:18:18,640 --> 00:18:21,960
indeed, it caressed in their gaze.’”.

268
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:26,000
He paused and took off his reading glasses again..

269
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:31,720
“It glimmered in their kindness to him,”, he repeated,, smiling..

270
00:18:31,720 --> 00:18:37,880
“Such a simple thing,, kindness.. Such a simple thing.. A nice word of

271
00:18:37,880 --> 00:18:43,880
encouragement given when needed., An act of friendship., A passing smile.”.

272
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:48,440
He closed the book,, put it down,, and leaned forward on the podium..

273
00:18:48,440 --> 00:18:54,520
“Children,, what I want to impart to you today is an understanding of the value of that

274
00:18:54,520 --> 00:18:59,480
simple thing called kindness.. And that’s all I want to leave you with today..

275
00:18:59,480 --> 00:19:04,240
I know I’m kind of infamous for my, um, verbosity.

276
00:19:04,240 --> 00:19:06,680
Here, everybody laughed again..

277
00:19:06,680 --> 00:19:10,240
I guess he knew he was known for his long speeches..

278
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:15,120
but what I want you,, my students,, to take away from your middle school

279
00:19:15,120 --> 00:19:19,960
experience,”," he continued,, "“is the sure knowledge that, in the

280
00:19:19,960 --> 00:19:25,760
future, you make for yourselves,, anything is possible.. If every single

281
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:32,360
person in this room made it a rule that wherever you are,, whenever you can,,

282
00:19:32,360 --> 00:19:38,760
you will try to act a little kinder than is necessary—, —the world really would be a better

283
00:19:38,760 --> 00:19:40,160
place..

284
00:19:40,160 --> 00:19:44,560
And if you do this,, if you act just a little kinder than is

285
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:52,640
necessary,, someone else,, somewhere,, someday, may recognize in you,, in every single

286
00:19:52,640 --> 00:19:56,320
one of you,, the face of God.”."

287
00:19:56,320 --> 00:19:58,760
He paused and shrugged..

288
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:02,760
"“Or whatever politically correct spiritual representative of universal

289
00:20:02,760 --> 00:20:07,320
goodness you happen to believe in,”," he added quickly,, smiling,.

290
00:20:07,320 --> 00:20:11,800
which got a lot of laughs and loads of applause,, especially from the back of

291
00:20:11,800 --> 00:20:16,200
the auditorium,, where the parents were sitting.

292
00:20:16,200 --> 00:20:22,040
Awards. I LIKED MR.. TUSHMAN’S speech,, but I have to admit:, I kind of zoned out

293
00:20:22,040 --> 00:20:24,840
a little during some of the other speeches..

294
00:20:24,840 --> 00:20:28,920
I tuned in again as Miss Reuben started reading off the names of the kids who’d

295
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:32,960
made the High Honor Roll because we were supposed to stand up when our names were

296
00:20:32,960 --> 00:20:37,400
called.. So, I waited and listened for my name as she went down the list

297
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:44,360
alphabetically. Reed Kingsley., Maya Markowitz., August Pullman.. I stood up..

298
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:47,880
Then when she finished reading off the names,, she asked all of us to face the

299
00:20:47,880 --> 00:20:51,080
audience and take a bow,, and everyone applauded..

300
00:20:51,080 --> 00:20:55,960
I had no idea where in that huge crowd my parents might be sitting.. All I could

301
00:20:55,960 --> 00:20:59,880
see were the flashes of light from people taking photos and parents waving

302
00:20:59,880 --> 00:21:07,680
at their kids.. I pictured Mom waving at me from somewhere, even though I I see her..

303
00:21:07,680 --> 00:21:11,880
Then Mr.. Tushman came back to the podium to present the medals for academic

304
00:21:11,880 --> 00:21:17,360
excellence,, and Jack was right. Zemina Chen won the gold medal for “overall

305
00:21:17,360 --> 00:21:20,000
academic excellence in the fifth grade.”.

306
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
Charlotte won the silver..

307
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:25,040
Charlotte also won a gold medal for music..

308
00:21:25,040 --> 00:21:29,160
Amos won the medal for overall excellence in sports,, which I was really

309
00:21:29,160 --> 00:21:34,280
happy about because, ever since the nature retreat,, I considered Amos to be

310
00:21:34,280 --> 00:21:36,840
like one of my best friends in school..

311
00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:40,920
But I was really,, really thrilled when Mr.. Tushman called out Summer’s name for

312
00:21:40,920 --> 00:21:43,600
the gold medal in creative writing..

313
00:21:43,600 --> 00:21:47,640
I saw Summer put her hand over her mouth when her name was called,, and when she

314
00:21:47,640 --> 00:21:52,120
walked up onto the stage,, I yelled, "Woohoo, Summer!”!"

315
00:21:52,120 --> 00:21:56,160
as loudly as I could,, though I don’t think she heard me..

316
00:21:56,160 --> 00:22:00,680
After the last name was called,, all the kids who just won awards stood next to

317
00:22:00,680 --> 00:22:04,920
each other on stage, and Mr.. Tushman said to the audience:,

318
00:22:04,920 --> 00:22:09,320
"“Ladies and gentlemen,, I am very honored to present to you this year’s Beecher

319
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:11,680
Prep School scholastic achievers..

320
00:22:11,680 --> 00:22:14,520
Congratulations to all of you!”."

321
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:18,120
I applauded as the kids on stage bowed..

322
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:21,600
I was so happy for Summer..

323
00:22:21,600 --> 00:22:24,040
"“The final award this morning,”," said Mr..

324
00:22:24,040 --> 00:22:27,920
Tushman, after the kids on stage had returned to their seats,,

325
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:31,960
"“is the Henry Ward Beecher medal to honor students who have been notable or

326
00:22:31,960 --> 00:22:35,600
exemplary in certain areas throughout the school year..

327
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:39,600
Typically,, this medal has been our way of acknowledging volunteerism or service

328
00:22:39,600 --> 00:22:41,240
to the school.”.

329
00:22:41,240 --> 00:22:45,360
I immediately figured Charlotte would get this medal because she organized the

330
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:47,000
coat drive this year,.

331
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,760
so, I kind of zoned out a bit again..

332
00:22:49,760 --> 00:22:55,280
I looked at my watch:, 10:::56.. I was getting hungry for lunch already..

333
00:22:55,280 --> 00:23:00,840
Henry Ward Beecher was,, of course,, the 19th century abolitionist— —and fiery

334
00:23:00,840 --> 00:23:05,720
sermonizer for human rights—, —after whom this school was named,”, Mr.. Tushman was

335
00:23:05,720 --> 00:23:08,560
saying when I started paying attention again..

336
00:23:08,560 --> 00:23:12,760
“While reading up on his life in preparation for this award,, I came up on

337
00:23:12,760 --> 00:23:16,680
a passage that he wrote that seemed particularly consistent with the themes

338
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:18,360
I touched on earlier,.

339
00:23:18,360 --> 00:23:21,880
themes I’ve been ruminating upon all year long..

340
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:27,560
Not just the nature of kindness,, but the nature of one’s kindness., The power of

341
00:23:27,560 --> 00:23:35,280
one’s friendship., The test of one’s character., The strength of one’s courage—”.

342
00:23:35,280 --> 00:23:37,400
And here the weirdest thing happened:.

343
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:42,200
Mr.. Tushman’s voice cracked a bit,, like he got all choked up.. He actually

344
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:45,400
cleared his throat and took a big sip of water..

345
00:23:45,400 --> 00:23:49,920
I started paying attention, for real now, to what he was saying..

346
00:23:49,920 --> 00:23:55,120
"“The strength of one’s courage,”." he repeated quietly,, nodding and smiling..

347
00:23:55,120 --> 00:23:58,280
He held up his right hand like he was counting off..

348
00:23:58,280 --> 00:24:02,400
"“Courage., Kindness., Friendship., Character..

349
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:06,560
These are the qualities that define us as human beings, and propel us,, on

350
00:24:06,560 --> 00:24:08,440
occasion,, to greatness..

351
00:24:08,440 --> 00:24:13,720
And this is what the Henry Ward Beecher medal is all about:, recognizing greatness..

352
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:18,680
“But how do we do that?? How do we measure something like greatness?? Again,, there’s

353
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:23,760
no yardstick for that kind of thing.. How do we even define it?? Well,, Beecher

354
00:24:23,760 --> 00:24:25,800
actually had an answer for that.”.

355
00:24:25,800 --> 00:24:29,520
He put his reading glasses on again,, leafed through a book,, and started to

356
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:34,760
read.. "“‘Greatness,’," wrote Beecher,, "‘lies not in being strong,, but in the right

357
00:24:34,760 --> 00:24:39,960
use of strength.. He is the greatest whose strength carries up the most hearts."

358
00:24:39,960 --> 00:24:44,680
And again,, out of the blue,, he got all choked up.. He put his two index fingers

359
00:24:44,680 --> 00:24:48,720
over his mouth for a second before continuing..

360
00:24:48,720 --> 00:24:53,200
"“‘He is the greatest,’”," he finally continued,, "“‘whose strength carries up

361
00:24:53,200 --> 00:24:56,480
the most hearts by the attraction of his own.’."

362
00:24:56,480 --> 00:25:03,360
Without further ado,, this year I am very proud to award the Henry Ward Beecher medal

363
00:25:03,360 --> 00:25:08,800
to the student whose quiet strength has carried up the most hearts..

364
00:25:08,800 --> 00:25:14,040
“So, will August Pullman please come up here to receive this award?

365
00:25:14,040 --> 00:25:15,440
Floating.

366
00:25:15,440 --> 00:25:17,400
PEOPLE STARTED APPLAUDING before Mr..

367
00:25:17,400 --> 00:25:20,840
Tushman’s words actually registered in my brain..

368
00:25:20,840 --> 00:25:26,160
I heard Maya,, who was next to me,, give a little happy scream when she heard my name,.

369
00:25:26,160 --> 00:25:30,160
and Miles,, who was on the other side of me,, patted my back..

370
00:25:30,160 --> 00:25:35,360
"“Stand up,! get up!”!" said kids all around me,. and I felt lots of hands pushing me

371
00:25:35,360 --> 00:25:42,080
upward out of my seat,, guiding me to the edge of the row,, patting my back,, high-fiving me..

372
00:25:42,080 --> 00:25:45,240
"“Way to go,, Auggie!”! “Nice going,, Auggie!”!"

373
00:25:45,240 --> 00:25:51,640
I even started hearing my name being chanted. "Auggie! Auggie! Auggie!"

374
00:25:51,640 --> 00:25:56,200
I looked back and saw Jack leading the chant,, fist in the air,, smiling, and

375
00:25:56,200 --> 00:26:03,080
signaling for me to keep going,. and Amos shouted through his hands, "Woohoo, little dude!"

376
00:26:03,080 --> 00:26:07,600
And I saw Summer smiling as I walked past her row,. and when she saw me look

377
00:26:07,600 --> 00:26:14,320
at her,, she gave me a secret little thumbs-up and mouthed the silent "“cool beans”" to me..

378
00:26:14,320 --> 00:26:17,720
I laughed and shook my head like I couldn’t believe it..

379
00:26:17,720 --> 00:26:20,000
I really couldn’t believe it..

380
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:21,880
I think I was smiling..

381
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:24,800
Maybe I was beaming,. I don’t know..

382
00:26:24,800 --> 00:26:29,000
As I walked up the aisle toward the stage,, all I saw was a blur of happy

383
00:26:29,000 --> 00:26:34,040
bright faces looking at me,, and hands clapping for me.. And I heard people

384
00:26:34,040 --> 00:26:39,320
yelling things out at me:. "“You deserve it,, Auggie!”! “Good for you,, Auggie!”!"

385
00:26:39,320 --> 00:26:44,320
I saw all my teachers in the aisle seats,, Mr.. Browne and Ms.. Petosa and Mr..

386
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:49,440
Roche and Mrs. Anatabi and Nurse Molly and all the others:. and they were

387
00:26:49,440 --> 00:26:54,600
cheering for me, woohooing and whistling..

388
00:26:54,600 --> 00:26:59,360
I felt like I was floating.. It was so weird.. Like the sun was shining full

389
00:26:59,360 --> 00:27:02,320
force on my face and the wind was blowing..

390
00:27:02,320 --> 00:27:04,920
As I got closer to the stage,, I saw Miss

391
00:27:04,920 --> 00:27:09,720
Reuben waving at me in the front row, and then next to her was Mrs.. G, who was

392
00:27:09,720 --> 00:27:11,680
crying hysterically—.

393
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:15,640
—a happy crying—. —smiling and clapping the whole time..

394
00:27:15,640 --> 00:27:20,400
And as I walked up the steps to the stage,, the most amazing thing happened:.

395
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:25,640
everyone started standing up.. Not just the front rows,, but the whole audience

396
00:27:25,640 --> 00:27:30,720
suddenly got up on their feet,. whooping,, hollering,, clapping like crazy.. It was a

397
00:27:30,720 --> 00:27:32,480
standing ovation..

398
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:33,960
For me..

399
00:27:33,960 --> 00:27:38,560
I walked across the stage to Mr.. Tushman, who shook my hand with both his hands

400
00:27:38,560 --> 00:27:42,000
and whispered in my ear:, "“Well done,, Auggie.”."

401
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:46,640
Then he placed the gold medal over my head,, just like they do in the Olympics,,

402
00:27:46,640 --> 00:27:50,760
and had me turn to face the audience.. It felt like I was watching myself in a

403
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:53,960
movie, almost,. like I was someone else..

404
00:27:53,960 --> 00:27:58,720
It was like that last scene in Star Wars Episode 4: A New Hope, when Luke

405
00:27:58,720 --> 00:28:04,680
Skywalker,, Han Solo,, and Chewbacca are being applauded for destroying the Death Star..

406
00:28:04,680 --> 00:28:08,360
I could almost hear the Star Wars theme music playing in my head as I sit on the

407
00:28:08,360 --> 00:28:09,640
stage..

408
00:28:09,640 --> 00:28:13,440
I wasn’t even sure why I was getting this medal,, really..

409
00:28:13,440 --> 00:28:15,480
No,, that’s not true..

410
00:28:15,480 --> 00:28:16,920
I knew why..

411
00:28:16,920 --> 00:28:20,800
It’s like people you see sometimes, and you can’t imagine what it would be like

412
00:28:20,800 --> 00:28:25,160
to be that person,. whether it’s somebody in a wheelchair or somebody who can’t

413
00:28:25,160 --> 00:28:29,440
talk.. Only, I know that I’m that person to other people,.

414
00:28:29,440 --> 00:28:33,160
maybe to every single person in that whole auditorium..

415
00:28:33,160 --> 00:28:37,560
To me,, though,, I’m just me.. An ordinary kid..

416
00:28:37,560 --> 00:28:39,720
But hey,, if they want to give me a medal

417
00:28:39,720 --> 00:28:43,200
for being me,, that’s okay.. I’ll take it..

418
00:28:43,200 --> 00:28:47,520
I didn’t destroy a Death Star or anything like that,, but I did just get

419
00:28:47,520 --> 00:28:53,560
through the fifth grade., And that’s not easy,, even if you’re not me.

420
00:28:53,560 --> 00:28:54,880
Pictures.

421
00:28:54,880 --> 00:28:58,720
AFTERWARD, THERE WAS a reception for the fifth and sixth graders under a huge

422
00:28:58,720 --> 00:29:01,080
white tent in the back of the school..

423
00:29:01,080 --> 00:29:04,720
All the kids found their parents,, and I didn’t mind at all when Mom and Dad

424
00:29:04,720 --> 00:29:08,440
hugged me like crazy,, or when Via wrapped her arms around me and swung me

425
00:29:08,440 --> 00:29:10,880
left and right about 20 times..

426
00:29:10,880 --> 00:29:15,800
Then Papa and Tata hugged me,, and Aunt Kate and Uncle Po,, and Uncle Ben—.

427
00:29:15,800 --> 00:29:19,280
—everyone kind of teary-eyed and wet-cheeked., But Miranda was the

428
00:29:19,280 --> 00:29:23,480
funniest:. she was crying more than anyone, and squeezed me so tight that

429
00:29:23,480 --> 00:29:28,400
Via had to practically pry her off of me,, which made them both laugh..

430
00:29:28,400 --> 00:29:32,320
Everyone started taking pictures of me and pulling out their Flips,, and then

431
00:29:32,320 --> 00:29:36,880
Dad got me,, Summer,, and Jack together for a group shot.. We put our arms around

432
00:29:36,880 --> 00:29:41,000
each other’s shoulders,, and for the first time I can remember,, I wasn’t even

433
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:45,440
thinking about my face.. I was just smiling a big, fat, happy smile for all

434
00:29:45,440 --> 00:29:47,880
the different cameras clicking away at me..

435
00:29:47,880 --> 00:29:52,320
Flash,, flash,, click,, click. smiling away as Jack’s parents and Summer’s mom

436
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:57,400
started clicking.. Then Reed and Maya came over.. Flash,, flash,, click,, click.

437
00:29:57,400 --> 00:30:00,360
And then Charlotte came over and asked if she could take a picture with us,, and

438
00:30:00,360 --> 00:30:04,400
we were like,, "“Sure,, of course!”." And then Charlotte’s parents were snapping

439
00:30:04,400 --> 00:30:08,000
away at our little group along with everyone else’s parents..

440
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:12,560
And the next thing I knew,, the two Maxes had come over,, and Henry and Miles, and

441
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:18,080
Savannah. Then Amos came over,, and Zamina. And we were all in this big,

442
00:30:18,080 --> 00:30:22,280
tight huddle as parents clicked away like we were on a red carpet somewhere..

443
00:30:22,280 --> 00:30:27,120
Luca., Isaiah., Nino., Pablo., Tristan., Ellie..

444
00:30:27,120 --> 00:30:29,880
I lost track of who else came over..

445
00:30:29,880 --> 00:30:34,400
Everybody,, practically.. All I knew for sure is that we were all laughing and

446
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:38,480
squeezing in tight against each other,, and no one seemed to care if it was my

447
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:44,200
face that was next to theirs or not.. In fact,, and I don’t mean to brag here,,

448
00:30:44,200 --> 00:30:49,400
but it kind of felt like everyone wanted to get close to me.

449
00:30:49,400 --> 00:30:50,800
The walk home.

450
00:30:50,800 --> 00:30:54,920
WE WALKED TO our house for cake and ice cream after the reception.. Jack and his

451
00:30:54,920 --> 00:30:59,480
parents and his little brother, Jamie., Summer and her mother., Uncle Po and Aunt

452
00:30:59,480 --> 00:31:05,560
Kate., Uncle Ben,, Tata and Papa, Justin and Via and Miranda., Mom and Dad..

453
00:31:05,560 --> 00:31:09,680
It was one of those great June days when the sky is completely blue and the sun

454
00:31:09,680 --> 00:31:14,360
is shining, but it isn’t so hot that you wish you were on the beach instead.. It

455
00:31:14,360 --> 00:31:16,240
was just the perfect day..

456
00:31:16,240 --> 00:31:22,560
Everyone was happy.. I still felt like I was floating,, the Star Wars hero music in my head..

457
00:31:22,560 --> 00:31:26,480
I walked with Summer and Jack, and we just couldn’t stop cracking up..

458
00:31:26,480 --> 00:31:30,760
Everything made us laugh.. We were in that giggly kind of mood where all

459
00:31:30,760 --> 00:31:34,280
someone has to do is look at you and you start laughing..

460
00:31:34,280 --> 00:31:38,320
I heard Dad’s voice up ahead and looked up.. He was telling everyone a funny

461
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:42,440
story as they walked down Amherst Road Avenue.. The grownups were all laughing,,

462
00:31:42,440 --> 00:31:47,560
too.. It was like Mom always said:, Dad could be a comedian.. I noticed Mom

463
00:31:47,560 --> 00:31:51,560
wasn’t walking with the group of grownups, so I looked behind me.. She was

464
00:31:51,560 --> 00:31:56,360
hanging back a bit,, smiling to herself like she was thinking of something sweet..

465
00:31:56,360 --> 00:31:58,000
She seemed happy..

466
00:31:58,000 --> 00:32:02,400
I took a few steps back and surprised her by hugging her as she walked.. She

467
00:32:02,400 --> 00:32:05,400
put her arm around me and gave me a squeeze..

468
00:32:05,400 --> 00:32:10,280
"“Thank you for making me go to school,”," I said quietly..

469
00:32:10,280 --> 00:32:14,360
She hugged me close and leaned down and kissed the top of my head..

470
00:32:14,360 --> 00:32:17,880
"“Thank you, Auggie,”," she answered softly..

471
00:32:17,880 --> 00:32:19,160
"“For what?”?"

472
00:32:19,160 --> 00:32:22,400
"“For everything you’ve given us,”," she said..

473
00:32:22,400 --> 00:32:24,600
"“For coming into our lives..

474
00:32:24,600 --> 00:32:26,360
For being you.”."

475
00:32:26,360 --> 00:32:29,320
She bent down and whispered in my ear..

476
00:32:29,320 --> 00:32:31,600
"“You really are a wonder,, Auggie..

477
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:34,520
You are a wonder."

